WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:10.450
Hey Wisecrack, Jared in the pale flesh again.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:14.850
Today, we’ve got an episode for you about
Pokemon Go, urban exploration, and some dead

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:15.850
French guy.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.580
You know, the usual.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.640
Pokemon Go temporarily gripped the world like
an epidemic.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.080
The augmented reality mobile game, just days
after its release, was installed on 5% of

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:31.130
all Android devices and even surpassed Tinder
in downloads. to say that we were gripped

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.479
by Pokemania all over again is an understatement.

00:00:34.479 --> 00:00:38.510
The media, and now Wisecrack, just wouldn’t
shut up about it.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.879
Pokemon Go-ers have discovered dead bodies,
been lured into being robbed, crashed their

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:46.190
cars, and even trolled the Westboro Baptist
church.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.370
At the same time, plenty of people were less
than enamored by Pokemon Go.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:54.909
Filmmaker Oliver Stone decried the game’s
rampant data collection as a new form of surveillance

00:00:54.909 --> 00:01:05.930
capitalism: Totalitarianism.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:10.480
So is Pokemon Go a nightmare confluence of
phone addiction and corporate surveillance?

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.480
Maybe.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.480
Let’s find out.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.970
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy
of Pokemon Go.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.060
And for once, no spoilers ahead.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.720
At it’s heart, Pokemon go is a game about
walking.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.660
You saunter over to a pokestop, dash towards
a raid, stampede towards a Dragonite, trespass

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.480
into a neighbor’s yard, stumble into a crime
scene, and so on.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.490
And while “walking” may seem as philosophically
unimportant as an Adam Sandler movie - you’d

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.800
be surprised.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.160
But before we can understand how Pokemon Go
radically changes how we interact with physical

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.920
spaces, we need a little history of walking.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:50.130
Unsurprisingly, walking played a prominent
role in society until the rise of the automobile.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:55.240
Besides getting people from point A to point
B, walking was a sort of sport in aristocratic

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.240
circles.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.300
As Frédéric Gros writes in his book “A
Philosophy of Walking, well-to-do men and

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:05.409
women would stroll around in fancy clothes,
occasionally striking a pose to woo potential

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:06.409
suitors.

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:11.060
It was kind of like the physical manifestation
of rich people selfies on Instagram.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:16.299
For others, walking was a holy matter: every
year scores of people would undergo a pilgrimage

00:02:16.299 --> 00:02:18.519
to various sanctified sites.

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:23.709
Meanwhile, philosophers like Friedrich Nietzsche
saw walking as an invaluable part of the philosophical

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:24.709
process.

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:30.079
Today, our walking, and movement in general,
still serves a social function, though it’s

00:02:30.079 --> 00:02:32.420
a little more pragmatic now.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:37.620
Our morning commute, running to the grocery
store, picking up Sonny Jim from soccer practice,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.290
most of these comings and goings are programmed
into our daily lives.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:46.680
We move from place to place, generally unaware
of our surroundings, and never venture far

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.340
from our planned route, all to fulfill our
societal obligations.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.599
As Gros writes, this kind of rushing around
is always in the service of “doing something,”

00:02:55.599 --> 00:02:58.359
and never to “just be.”

00:02:58.359 --> 00:03:03.450
Walking, according to Gros, in its purest
form is about doing nothing, which allows

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:08.690
us to “rediscover the simple joy of existing,”
a kind of child-like wonderment with the world

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:10.040
surrounding us.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.439
Some might argue that Pokemon Go, according
to this logic, follows the “always doing

00:03:14.439 --> 00:03:16.010
something” logic of our time.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:20.689
Frantically running from Pokestop to Pokestop,
chasing down Legendary raids, and battling

00:03:20.689 --> 00:03:24.749
gyms isn’t exactly the kind of exploration
Gros is writing about.

00:03:24.749 --> 00:03:29.749
We’ll tackle that in just a second, but
first, let’s talk about space.

00:03:29.749 --> 00:03:34.279
Every street, building and open space carries
with it an assumed social code.

00:03:34.279 --> 00:03:38.669
You wouldn’t behave in an office building
in the same way you would a concert, you don’t

00:03:38.669 --> 00:03:43.059
play football on a freeway, and many would
say it’s in poor taste to wear your Black

00:03:43.059 --> 00:03:45.239
Sabbath t-shirt to church.

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:48.389
But those social codes change with time and
culture.

00:03:48.389 --> 00:03:53.290
Public bathing used to be a thing, bars used
to be a place to find a date, and rivers used

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:57.469
to be a fine place to drop trou and do your
business.

00:03:57.469 --> 00:04:02.659
Pokemon Go, and the technology it uses, might
change everything by radically recoding the

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:04.049
space we live in.

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:10.069
The game has literally mapped onto reality
an entirely new set of social codes.

00:04:10.069 --> 00:04:11.309
Parks for hiking?

00:04:11.309 --> 00:04:14.569
More like a sweet place to battle a Mewtwo.

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:16.359
Churches for worship?

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:18.480
Pokeballs for days.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.260
Panicked crowd all running in same direction?

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.040
Could be a zombie apocalypse, but it could
be an unown.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.700
Museums to memorialize the millions of people
slaughtered?

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:28.700
Pokemon Go in holocaust museum?

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.650
More like… ok, seriously guys, don’t play
Pokemon in the Holocaust Museum.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:37.860
In short, Pokemon Go has disrupted how we
perceive space.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:43.470
There is a common fear with Pokemon Go: Should
we really trust a massive corporation with

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.540
such intimate knowledge of where we’re going
and how we’re getting there?

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:52.030
Is it a little scary that a company can so
artfully tell us where to go?

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.710
Niantic has integrated sponsored pokestops,
and Starbucks, for instance, as more than

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:58.130
happy to oblige.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:02.540
The idea is to get you to buy a Pumpkin Spice
Latte once you’ve decided to take a break

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.650
from catching mythical monsters.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:09.940
So far, aside from some sponsored Pokestops,
Niantic has taken the high road: organizing

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:12.500
things like beach cleanups for Earth Day.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.680
But that doesn’t mean it can’t go downhill
fairly quickly.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:21.290
What if you could pay Niantic a couple million
dollars to have rare pokemon spawn at your

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:25.230
political rally, or worse, your friend’s
improv show?

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:30.290
For French philosopher Guy Debord, modern
capitalism has neatly organized time and physical

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:31.290
space.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.851
Where we are, where we’re going, and when
we’re getting there is a function of of

00:05:34.851 --> 00:05:40.450
the totalizing control the economy plays in
our lives- we travel through life mesmerized

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:41.880
by our commodity-filled environment.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:47.810
We go to work to make money, to a leisure
activity to spend money, go home, go to sleep,

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:52.740
and repeat, with a sort of systemic pressure
to do this in a more and more productive way

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.880
- work harder, enjoy more, sleep less.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:57.610
You get the idea.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:01.330
For some, Pokemon Go easily fits this dystopic
narrative.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:06.810
Yesterday we were mesmerized by billboards
and where they told us go, probably some TGI

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:07.810
Fridays.

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:12.420
Today we’re mesmerized by our phone screens
and and where they tell us to go.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:16.190
What is missing from all of this is chance,
randomness and discovery.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:21.290
But before you think we’re being overly
paranoid edgelords, those who decry Pokemon

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:27.140
Go as the harbinger of some dystopic augmented
reality are only half right.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.340
Pokemon Go, as Edwin Montoya Zorrilla writes
in the Hong Kong Review of Books, resembles

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:37.650
“dérive,” a revolutionary strategy proposed
by Debord to combat the entrenched and alienated

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.110
ways in which we move through cities.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:44.950
Dérive, which is French for “drifting”,
means abandoning the pre-programmed way in

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:50.390
which we interact with cities by performing
a sort of experiment in space: wandering around

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:51.390
aimlessly.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:55.620
Debord encourages people to walk around, be
drawn in by their surroundings,interrogate

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.750
them, and to give themselves up to randomness.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.050
And that’s kind of what Pokemon Go does.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:07.810
Players venture to locations they never would
have dreamed of going to -- namely, outside.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:11.120
But if you’re anything like me, you’ve
discovered new neighborhoods, new landmarks,

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:15.460
and even, and this one’s a shocker, made
friends.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:17.360
In the meatspace.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.130
Pokemon Go is combating the kind of social
alienation that our culture actively promotes.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:26.240
While most games have you glued to a screen
with headphones on, Pokemon Go encourages

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.920
you to explore and make new friends.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:32.450
After the raid system was introduced, you
could battle boss-level Pokemon for a limited

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:34.430
time with friends or strangers.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:38.750
They also recently rolled out trading, adding
new ways to meet new people.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.240
Psychologists have even noticed that the game
was helping their patients with anxiety and

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.010
depression by getting them out of the house
and socializing.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:51.230
In this way, Pokemon Go actively recodes the
space around us in a positive way.

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:56.780
In might not be exactly the kind of revolutionary
walking that Debord envisioned, or the useless

00:07:56.780 --> 00:08:01.480
kind of walking Gros writes about, but it’s
also better than the alternative that our

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.070
tech-obsessed society offers us.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:09.060
Who knows - maybe Pokemon Go is a gateway
drug for hiking and social interaction?

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.280
What do you think Wisecrack?

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:15.230
Is Pokemon Go society’s savior, or a harbinger
of dismal times to come?

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:48.890
Or is it just a game about animals

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:52.160
in balls?

00:08:52.160 --> 00:09:05.450
Let us know know in the comments.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:15.350
Thanks for watching, y’all.

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:19.269
peace.

